INT. LUMEN LABS - OPEN OFFICE - MORNING

Whiteboards covered in launch checklists. NADIA VASQUEZ, 30s, founder, paces with a tablet while OMAR FASEEH, 30s, lead engineer, trails her with a laptop open to a wall of error logs.

NADIA
Six hours to the livestream, Omar. Tell me the good news first, then whatever this face is about.

OMAR
There isn't a good-news version today. The sync service is dropping about one request in forty under real load, and we've never load-tested past half our expected launch traffic.

NADIA
One in forty is a rounding error. I need you focused on the demo path, not chasing a number that small.

INT. LUMEN LABS - NADIA'S OFFICE - MORNING

Nadia paces behind her desk, phone on speaker, a countdown clock visible on her monitor.

INVESTOR (V.O.)
(filtered)
We've got three outlets confirmed to cover the launch live. This needs to go flawlessly, Nadia. You understand what's riding on today.

NADIA
It will. We've tested every demo path a dozen times over. Today is the easy part.

She hangs up and stares at the countdown a beat longer than she'd like anyone to see.

INT. LUMEN LABS - SERVER ROOM - MORNING

Omar sits cross-legged on the floor between two racks, running a script that spits out a growing column of red text.

OMAR
(to himself)
One in forty at low load. What does that number do to us at ten times the traffic.

He doesn't like the answer forming in his head and keeps digging anyway.

INT. LUMEN LABS - OPEN OFFICE - MIDDAY

Omar corners Nadia by the whiteboard, laptop turned toward her.

OMAR
I ran the numbers at projected launch traffic. That one-in-forty becomes a full outage window somewhere in the first ten minutes of the stream.

NADIA
You said the same thing about the pricing page bug in March and we launched fine. I can't rebuild the sync layer six hours before three outlets go live.

OMAR
You also didn't listen about the pricing page bug, Nadia. It broke checkout for two days. I'd rather be wrong loudly now than quietly right later.

INT. LUMEN LABS - KITCHEN - MIDDAY

Marketing intern PIP OYELARAN, 20s, restocks a fridge with branded sparkling water, eavesdropping shamelessly on Omar and Nadia's raised voices down the hall.

PIP
(to a coworker passing by)
I give it even odds they're either fixing this in the next four hours or one of them is quitting on camera at five.

INT. LUMEN LABS - CONFERENCE ROOM - AFTERNOON

A full dress rehearsal of the livestream in progress. Nadia delivers her opening line smoothly, then the demo screen behind her freezes mid-sentence, frozen on a loading spinner.

NADIA
(under her breath, to the room)
That is exactly the failure mode Omar was describing three hours ago.

STAGE MANAGER
We can cut around it in editing for the rehearsal, but live we don't get a cut.

INT. LUMEN LABS - OPEN OFFICE - AFTERNOON

Nadia finds Omar still at the whiteboard, dry-erase marker in hand, diagramming the sync path.

NADIA
Fine. You were right about the pricing page and you might be right about this too. What do you actually need from me in the next four hours?

OMAR
Time in the server room, no interruptions, and someone to keep the marketing team out of my hair while I work.

NADIA
Take the time. I'll handle marketing myself.

INT. LUMEN LABS - SERVER ROOM - AFTERNOON

Omar works alone, headphones on, rewriting a retry policy line by line while a live counter of failed requests slowly ticks down beside his terminal.

OMAR
(to himself)
Come on. One in forty becomes one in four thousand or this whole thing isn't worth doing tonight.

The counter finally settles near zero and holds there.

INT. LUMEN LABS - STUDIO SET - EVENING

Cameras roll. Nadia stands at a podium under bright light, calm and rehearsed, a live viewer count climbing in the corner of a monitor only the crew can see.

NADIA
Today we're showing you something we've rebuilt from the ground up, live, no safety net. Let's begin.

INT. LUMEN LABS - STUDIO SET - EVENING (CONTINUOUS)

The demo screen flickers once, the exact stutter from rehearsal, and then steadies. In the control booth, Omar watches his retry counter absorb the spike without a single dropped request.

PIP
(whispering, to Omar)
It held. It actually held.

OMAR
(quiet, not looking away from the monitor)
Barely. Ask me again in four thousand and one requests.

INT. LUMEN LABS - OPEN OFFICE - NIGHT

The livestream ends to applause from the crew. Nadia finds Omar still at his monitor instead of celebrating with everyone else.

NADIA
You fixed in four hours what I was ready to gamble the whole launch on ignoring. I owe you an apology, not just a thank-you.

OMAR
Apology accepted. Next time, though, I'd rather you hear the loud wrong-sounding warning before we're six hours out.

NADIA
Deal. I'll even write it on the whiteboard so I can't pretend I didn't see it.

EXT. LUMEN LABS - ROOFTOP - NIGHT

Nadia and Omar sit on the ledge overlooking the city, two warm sparkling waters between them, the office lights below finally starting to thin out for the night.

NADIA
One in forty. I'm never going to hear that phrase again without breaking into a cold sweat.

OMAR
Good. That's exactly the reaction I was going for the first time I said it this morning.
